{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZVGAVBtwEZo5iWnTgEx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote 8 test statistics for the incumbent model `personal_ideal_alternation` into `iter_1/critique/test_stats/`. I ran each one once on the observed responses: each returns a finite number in about 10 ms or less, well within the time limits. I haven't run the posterior-predictive check itself, since the pipeline does that, so there are no p-values yet.\n\n| Statistic | What it measures | Observed |\n|---|---|---|\n| `one_switch_vs_constant` | How often people pick the one-switch sequence over an all-same one (e.g. HHHHHHHT vs HHHHHHHH) | 0.864 |\n| `balance_pref_matched_alternation` | Among irregular pairs with near-equal switching rates but different H/T counts, how often people pick the more balanced one | 0.581 |\n| `perfect_alternation_choice` | How often people pick a perfectly alternating sequence over a streaky one (e.g. HTHTHTHT vs HHHHHHHT) | 0.743 |\n| `participant_spread_alt_pref` | Spread across people in how often each picks the more-switching sequence | 0.262 |\n| `maxrun_effect_beyond_alternation` | Whether the longest run affects choices beyond what the switching rate explains (regression coefficient) | 0.079 |\n| `short_vs_long_alt_pref_gap` | Preference for the more-switching sequence at length ≤7 minus at length 8 | 0.034 |\n| `left_choice_rate` | Overall rate of choosing the left sequence (a side bias) | 0.525 |\n| `side_repetition_rate` | How often people choose the same side as on their previous trial | 0.511 |\n\nThe incumbent scores each sequence only by its proportion of switches between consecutive flips. So the statistics target what that leaves out: H/T balance, run length, and side-choice habits. Two also test its specific predictions: a split across people on perfectly alternating sequences, and wide individual differences.\n\nThe first version of `perfect_alternation_choice` compared perfect alternators with irregular sequences, but no such pairs exist in these data. It fell back to returning 0.5. I changed it to compare against streaky sequences, which is the only kind a perfect alternator is paired with here."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1252,"cache_read_input_tokens":52188,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1252,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":730,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e22b576b-2776-4071-a7c7-200b113ae8a0","uuid":"375119fc-3bbd-4a1c-8063-752fff625436","timestamp":"2026-09-30T10:45:10.933Z","request_id":"req_011CfZVG9yAvH8PwtFFxtdWW"}
